    <h1 style="margin-top: 2rem; font-size: 3rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em;">History of the Deck</h1>
    
    <h2>Origins and Errata</h2>
    <p>
        The deck’s lineage traces back to Standard of the early 2000s. In Premodern, Replenish has sustained a resurgence as a powerful and flexible strategy tunable toward aggressive combo kills, grinding midrange play, or long-game control.
    </p>
    <p>
        A critical rules change elevated the deck: the removal of old errata on the Parallax cards. After errata was reversed to match the printed cards, Parallax Wave and Parallax Tide now create delayed triggered abilities. If a Wave or Tide leaves play before the removal abilities resolve, the return trigger is severed—permanently exiling creatures or lands. Paired with Opalescence, this supercharged the deck’s combo lines.
    </p>

    <h2>The Parallax Tide Ban (January 2026)</h2>
    <p>
        On January 18, 2026, format curator Martin Berlin banned Parallax Tide. Thirty percent of top-8 decks across major 2025 tournaments were Tide-based archetypes. Replenish posted a 59.5% win rate in 2025—and when filtered against top-tier competition, it led the format at 54.9%.
    </p>
    <p>
        The community wrote Replenish off. The prevailing take: without Tide’s one-sided Armageddon, Replenish was a worse Enchantress.
    </p>
    <p>
        Those predictions have not held up. For Brian Siu, over 248 post-ban matches, the deck has maintained a 72.3% win rate across the full metagame. On MTGO, Replenish has one of the most 5-0 league finishes. At the 131-player Duress Crew New England Winter Regional - one of the first major paper events post-ban - Replenish placed 1st and 2nd, with three copies in the top 8.
    </p>
